    @{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Generics in Java";
}

<div class="card border-0 shadow-sm">

    <div class="card-body p-4 p-lg-5">

        <div class="mb-4">
            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">Generics in Java</h2>

            <p class="lead text-muted mb-0">
                Learn how Java Generics provide type safety, reusable
                components, generic classes, methods, interfaces, wildcards,
                bounds, and type inference.
            </p>
        </div>


        <!-- INTRODUCTION -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">What are Generics?</h3>

            <p>
                Generics allow classes, interfaces, and methods to operate
                with parameterized types. They help catch type-related
                errors at compile time instead of relying on runtime casts.
            </p>

            <p>
                Generics are widely used throughout the Java Collections
                Framework.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;String&gt; names =
    new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

names.add("Amit");
names.add("Neha");</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- WITHOUT GENERICS -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Before Generics</h3>

            <p>
                Older Java code could store objects without specifying their
                intended type.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List values = new ArrayList();

values.add("Java");
values.add(100);

String text = (String) values.get(0);</code></pre>

            </div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Raw collections lose useful compile-time type checking and
                often require explicit casts.
            </p>

        </section>


        <!-- WITH GENERICS -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Using Generics</h3>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;String&gt; values =
    new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

values.add("Java");

String text = values.get(0);</code></pre>

            </div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The compiler knows that the list contains Strings, so an
                explicit cast is not required.
            </p>

        </section>


        <!-- TYPE SAFETY -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Type Safety</h3>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;String&gt; names =
    new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

names.add("Java");

// Compile-time error:
// names.add(100);</code></pre>

            </div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Generics prevent incompatible values from being added to a
                parameterized collection.
            </p>

        </section>


        <!-- GENERIC CLASS -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Generic Classes</h3>

            <p>
                A generic class declares one or more type parameters.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>class Box&lt;T&gt; {

    private T value;

    void set(T value) {

        this.value = value;

    }

    T get() {

        return value;

    }

}</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- USING GENERIC CLASS -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Using a Generic Class
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Box&lt;String&gt; stringBox =
    new Box&lt;&gt;();

stringBox.set("Hello");

String text = stringBox.get();

System.out.println(text);</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="code-block mt-3">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Hello</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- DIFFERENT TYPES -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Same Generic Class with Different Types
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Box&lt;Integer&gt; numberBox =
    new Box&lt;&gt;();

numberBox.set(100);

Box&lt;Double&gt; decimalBox =
    new Box&lt;&gt;();

decimalBox.set(10.5);</code></pre>

            </div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The same generic class can be reused with different reference
                types.
            </p>

        </section>


        <!-- MULTIPLE TYPE PARAMETERS -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Multiple Type Parameters
            </h3>

            <p>
                A generic class can declare multiple type parameters.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>class Pair&lt;K, V&gt; {

    private K key;

    private V value;

    Pair(K key, V value) {

        this.key = key;
        this.value = value;

    }

    K getKey() {

        return key;

    }

    V getValue() {

        return value;

    }

}</code></pre>

            </div>
            

        </section>


        <!-- USING PAIR -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Using Multiple Type Parameters
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Pair&lt;Integer, String&gt; student =
    new Pair&lt;&gt;(101, "Amit");

System.out.println(
    student.getKey()
);

System.out.println(
    student.getValue()
);</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- GENERIC METHOD -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Generic Methods
            </h3>

            <p>
                A method can declare its own type parameter independently of
                the class.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>class Utility {

    static &lt;T&gt; void print(T value) {

        System.out.println(value);

    }

}</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- GENERIC METHOD USAGE -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Calling a Generic Method
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Utility.print("Java");

Utility.print(100);

Utility.print(10.5);</code></pre>

            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Java
100
10.5</code></pre>
</div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Java can infer the appropriate type argument from the method
                invocation.
            </p>

        </section>


        <!-- GENERIC INTERFACE -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Generic Interfaces
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>interface Repository&lt;T&gt; {

    void save(T value);

    T find();

}

class StringRepository
    implements Repository&lt;String&gt; {

    private String value;

    public void save(String value) {

        this.value = value;

    }

    public String find() {

        return value;

    }

}</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- BOUNDED TYPES -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Bounded Type Parameters
            </h3>

            <p>
                A type parameter can be restricted using an upper bound.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>class Calculator {

    static &lt;T extends Number&gt;
    double doubleValue(T value) {

        return value.doubleValue();

    }

}</code></pre>

            </div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Here, <code>T</code> must be a subtype of
                <code>Number</code>.
            </p>

        </section>


        <!-- MULTIPLE BOUNDS -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Multiple Bounds
            </h3>

            <p>
                A type parameter can have multiple bounds. A class, when
                present, must come first, followed by interfaces.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>&lt;T extends Number &amp; Comparable&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- WILDCARDS -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Wildcards
            </h3>

            <p>
                The wildcard <code>?</code> represents an unknown type.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;?&gt; values;</code></pre>

            </div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                A wildcard is useful when code needs to work with a collection
                whose exact element type is not known.
            </p>

        </section>


        <!-- UNBOUNDED WILDCARD -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Unbounded Wildcard
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>static void printList(
    List&lt;?&gt; values
) {

    for (Object value : values) {

        System.out.println(value);

    }

}</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- UPPER BOUNDED -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Upper-Bounded Wildcard
            </h3>

            <p>
                An upper-bounded wildcard uses <code>? extends Type</code>.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;? extends Number&gt; numbers;</code></pre>

            </div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Such a reference can point to a list of Number or any subtype
                of Number.
            </p>

        </section>


        <!-- LOWER BOUNDED -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Lower-Bounded Wildcard
            </h3>

            <p>
                A lower-bounded wildcard uses <code>? super Type</code>.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;? super Integer&gt; values;

values.add(10);
values.add(20);</code></pre>

            </div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                This is useful when a method needs to safely consume values
                of a particular type.
            </p>

        </section>


        <!-- PECS -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                PECS Principle
            </h3>

            <div class="alert alert-info border-0">

                <strong>PECS:</strong>

                Producer Extends, Consumer Super.

            </div>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Use <code>? extends T</code> when a generic structure
                    produces values of type T.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Use <code>? super T</code> when a generic structure
                    consumes values of type T.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </section>


        <!-- DIAMOND -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Diamond Operator
            </h3>

            <p>
                The diamond operator <code>&lt;&gt;</code> allows the compiler
                to infer generic type arguments in many object creation
                expressions.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;String&gt; names =
    new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- RAW TYPES -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Raw Types
            </h3>

            <p>
                A raw type omits the generic type argument.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List values = new ArrayList();</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="alert alert-warning mt-3">

                Raw types are mainly retained for compatibility with
                pre-generics Java code. New code should normally use
                parameterized types.

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- TYPE ERASURE -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Type Erasure
            </h3>

            <p>
                Java implements generics primarily through type erasure.
                Generic type information used for compile-time checking is
                generally erased from ordinary class and method signatures at
                runtime.
            </p>

            <p>
                This provides compatibility with older Java bytecode while
                still giving developers compile-time type safety.
            </p>

        </section>


        <!-- CANNOT USE PRIMITIVES -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Generics and Primitive Types
            </h3>

            <p>
                Java type arguments must be reference types, not primitive
                types.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>// Invalid:
// List&lt;int&gt; values;

// Correct:
List&lt;Integer&gt; values =
    new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();</code></pre>

            </div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                Wrapper classes such as <code>Integer</code>,
                <code>Double</code>, and <code>Boolean</code> are used when
                primitive values need to participate in generic APIs.
            </p>

        </section>


        <!-- AUTOBOXING -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Generics and Autoboxing
            </h3>

            <p>
                Java can automatically convert between primitive values and
                their corresponding wrapper types in many contexts.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;Integer&gt; numbers =
    new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

numbers.add(10);

int value = numbers.get(0);</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- GENERIC STACK -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Practical Example: Generic Stack
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>class Stack&lt;T&gt; {

    private List&lt;T&gt; items =
        new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

    void push(T item) {

        items.add(item);

    }

    T pop() {

        return items.remove(
            items.size() - 1
        );

    }

    boolean isEmpty() {

        return items.isEmpty();

    }

}</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- STACK USAGE -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Using the Generic Stack
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Stack&lt;String&gt; stack =
    new Stack&lt;&gt;();

stack.push("Java");
stack.push("Spring");

System.out.println(stack.pop());</code></pre>

            </div>

            <div class="code-block mt-3">

                <div class="code-lang-tag">Output</div>

                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Spring</code></pre>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- BENEFITS -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Benefits of Generics
            </h3>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Provides compile-time type safety.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Reduces explicit casting.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Makes reusable classes and methods possible.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Improves readability of APIs.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Works naturally with Java collections.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </section>


        <!-- BEST PRACTICES -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Generics Best Practices
            </h3>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Prefer parameterized types over raw types.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Use meaningful type parameter names such as
                    <code>T</code>, <code>K</code>, and <code>V</code> where
                    appropriate.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Use bounded wildcards when they express the intended API
                    relationship.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Follow the PECS principle for producer and consumer APIs.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Avoid unnecessary unchecked casts and raw-type usage.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </section>


        <!-- INTERVIEW -->

        <section class="mb-5">

            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Interview Questions
            </h3>

            <div class="accordion" id="genericQuestions">

                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#genericQuestionOne">

                            What are Generics in Java?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="genericQuestionOne"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#genericQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            Generics allow types to be parameterized, enabling
                            reusable and type-safe classes, interfaces, and
                            methods.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#genericQuestionTwo">

                            What is the benefit of Generics?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="genericQuestionTwo"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#genericQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            Generics provide compile-time type checking,
                            reduce casts, and allow reusable type-safe
                            components.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#genericQuestionThree">

                            What is a wildcard?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="genericQuestionThree"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#genericQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            The <code>?</code> wildcard represents an unknown
                            generic type. It can be unbounded or constrained
                            using <code>extends</code> or
                            <code>super</code>.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#genericQuestionFour">

                            What is PECS?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="genericQuestionFour"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#genericQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            PECS stands for Producer Extends, Consumer Super.
                            It is a common guideline for choosing between
                            upper- and lower-bounded wildcards.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>


                <div class="accordion-item">

                    <h2 class="accordion-header">

                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#genericQuestionFive">

                            Can Generics use primitive types?

                        </button>

                    </h2>

                    <div id="genericQuestionFive"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#genericQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">

                            No. Generic type arguments must be reference
                            types. Wrapper classes such as
                            <code>Integer</code> are used instead of
                            <code>int</code>.

                        </div>

                    </div>

                </div>

            </div>

        </section>


        <!-- SUMMARY -->

        <section class="mb-4">

            <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

                <h5 class="fw-bold">Summary</h5>

                <p class="mb-0">
                    Generics provide compile-time type safety and reusable
                    parameterized components in Java. Important concepts
                    include generic classes and methods, bounded type
                    parameters, wildcards, PECS, type inference, raw types,
                    and type erasure.
                </p>

            </div>

        </section>


